course: dictionary
lesson: 7752
title: Punishment and fairness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A punishment is something unpleasant that happens to someone because they broke a rule or did something wrong. Look at the words around it to work this out: «Rory missed football practice as a punishment, and kept looking at Finn.» The words «as a punishment» tell you that missing practice was the unpleasant result of doing something wrong.
explain: Punishments can be small, like losing playtime, or bigger, like a detention. A punishment that matches what was done is a fair punishment. «Letting someone else take your punishment takes away their good name and their time.» The author is showing that taking the blame for someone else is not fair on them.
explain: Punishments also bring feelings. «Feeling cross and hard-done-by after a punishment is normal.» Hard-done-by means you feel it was unfair to you. To check your meaning, put it into the sentence: "Rory missed practice as an unpleasant result of breaking a rule" still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: A punishment is something unpleasant done to someone because they did something wrong.
fact: «Punishment» can also mean the act of punishing someone.
fact: A fair punishment matches what was done wrong.
fact: Useful phrases: «as a punishment», «escape punishment», and «crime and punishment».
fact: In stories, the villain often gets a punishment at the end.
exercise: «Rory missed football practice as a punishment.» What is a punishment? | Something that makes you laugh | Something unpleasant done because someone did something wrong | *A prize for winning a game | A kind of football practice
exercise: «Finn hid in the shed to escape punishment.» What does escape punishment mean? | Ask for a fine to be paid | *Avoid the unpleasant result of breaking a rule | Ask the teacher for a bigger punishment | Get a prize for hiding well
exercise: Mia says a punishment is always just a telling-off from the teacher. Why is that not quite right? | Punishments are only for grown-ups | Punishments are always fun | *A punishment can be losing playtime, a detention or paying a fine | A punishment is always a shout
test: «Feeling cross and hard-done-by after a punishment is normal.» What does punishment mean here? | A reward for good work | A lesson about maths | A game played in class | *Something unpleasant done because someone broke a rule
test: In a story, the villain often gets a punishment at the end. What makes a punishment fair? | One that is never given | One that is bigger than the crime | One that is given without any reason | *One that matches what the villain did wrong
exam: Which sentence uses punishment correctly? | The punishment was a sunny day in the park | Rory won a punishment for scoring a goal | *Rory missed football practice as a punishment | Punishment is a kind of football ball
